course: dictionary
lesson: 2675
title: Dialogue: the words people speak
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Dialogue is the words that characters speak to each other. You can often find its meaning from the words around it. «In written dialogue, speech marks go around the words spoken, and each new speaker starts a new line.» The speech marks show you exactly which words are spoken aloud, so dialogue must mean the spoken words of the characters.
explain: Dialogue can also mean a conversation between two or more people. Look for clues like two people talking, or a line that starts a new speaker. The sentence about hooks says, «Common hooks are dialogue, action, a sound word, a question, a surprising fact and a strange detail.» Here dialogue is one of the ways a story can grab the reader, so it is a tool a writer can use.
explain: Dialogue can also be a formal talk. «Countries hold a dialogue to make peace.» The words make peace tell you this is a serious talk between groups or countries to solve a problem. When a word has more than one meaning, read the whole sentence and ask which meaning fits best.
fact: Dialogue means the words that characters speak in a story, play or film.
fact: A dialogue is a conversation between two or more people.
fact: Dialogue can also mean a formal talk between groups or countries to solve a problem.
fact: In written dialogue, speech marks go around the words spoken, and each new speaker starts a new line.
fact: In US English, the spelling is dialog, but in British English it is dialogue.
exercise: What is dialogue in a story, play or film? | *The words the characters speak to each other | A formal talk between two countries | A small window on a computer screen | The pictures on the cover of the book
exercise: Ben and Sam chat about the match on the bus. What is this conversation an example of? | A list of story hooks | A dialogue box that asks a question | A formal talk to solve a problem | *A dialogue, a conversation between two or more people
exercise: «Countries hold a dialogue to make peace.» What kind of dialogue is this? | *A formal talk between countries to solve a problem | A speech in a film | A question on a screen | A character's spoken words in a play
test: In written dialogue, where do speech marks go? | *Around the words spoken | Around each paragraph | Around the title of the story | Around the writer's name
test: What is the US spelling of dialogue? | dialoge | *dialog | dialogs | diologue
exam: When writing dialogue, what should you do for each new speaker? | *Start a new line | Keep all the speakers on the same line | Write the words in capital letters | Put speech marks around the title
